.TH CHMAP_INIT_CH 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_init_ch \- create a hash map with a custom hashing procedure into an already declared handle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_init_ch(hm_name, custom_hashing_proc);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a hash map that hashes its keys with custom_hashing_proc instead
of with the map's own hashing for the declared key type, and assigns it
to hm_name. The caller must declare the handle first, with
chmap_declare(3) or chmap_declare_scoped(3). The key and value types are
not arguments here: the macro reads them from the companion type
variables that the declare macro created.

This macro is chmap_init(3) plus a hashing procedure, and it uses the
default allocator. chmap_init_mp(3) replaces the allocator instead, and
chmap_init_full(3) takes both.

A custom procedure changes only how the map turns a key into a number.
It changes neither the implementation strategy that the map picks nor
which keys the map considers equal.

The one-step equivalents are chmap_construct_ch(3) and
chmap_construct_ch_scoped(3).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name              The already declared map handle to create the map into
custom_hashing_proc  A ccol_hashing_proc_t, or NULL to use the map's own
                     hashing for the declared key type
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro expands to a statement and does not expand to an
expression.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM HASHING"
.nf
A custom procedure receives a pointer to the key bytes and their length,
and returns an unsigned long. It must return the same value for two keys
that the map considers equal; for a char * key, equal means equal
contents, not an equal pointer. For a fixed-width key type, the length is
the map's own fixed key width, and for a string or other buffer-like key
it is the byte length that the caller gave. The procedure can therefore
always hash exactly that number of bytes, and needs no NUL terminator and
no length prefix of its own.

The return value does not need to avalanche, because the map finalizes
the value that a custom procedure returns with its own bit-mixing step
before it reduces the value to a slot or a bucket. A procedure that puts
all of its entropy in the low bits, such as a sum, a small identifier or
a truncated checksum, therefore still spreads across the table. The
contract is only
"any function of the key that respects the map's notion of key equality";
there is no rule about which end of the result carries the entropy.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails
Uses the default allocator and CCOL_DEFAULT_INITIAL_BUCKET_ARRAY_SIZE (16)
buckets
The map canonicalizes a float or double key before the procedure sees it,
so a custom procedure never gets a raw -0.0
A long double key is the one case where a custom procedure changes key
handling; chmap_create_full(3) states what such a procedure has to do
The procedure must stay callable for the whole life of the map
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
unsigned long hash_bytes(const void *p, size_t size);

chmap_declare(scores, char *, int);
chmap_init_ch(scores, hash_bytes);
int score = 42;
chmap_insert(scores, "alice", score);
chmap_destroy(scores);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_init (3),
.BR chmap_init_mp (3),
.BR chmap_init_full (3),
.BR chmap_declare (3),
.BR chmap_construct_ch (3),
.BR chmap_create_full (3),
.BR chmap_destroy (3)
